## Local first

- Run everything that can run locally first: Vitest, Node tests, native
  Worker controls, the local Bunny deployment selection, local Ruby, local
  SQLite/libSQL/MySQL. A hosted run is only for what local runs cannot show.
- Passing tests close tasks. Never delete, skip or weaken a test to get a pass.
  Any change to an existing test's assertions is called out in the commit
  message and gets independent review.

## Hosted Bunny tests cost real money

Published prices (from [cost-model.md](cost-model.md), read 2026-10-14):
$0.20 per started million Edge Scripting requests, $0.02 per started 1,000
CPU-seconds, $0.30 per billion Database rows read, $0.01 per GB CDN bandwidth,
and a $1.00 account minimum.

Measured cost per run: the test lead records the real charge of every hosted
run in the log below, from Bunny's billing/usage view or the account's past
charges, before and after the run. Until a measured figure exists, estimate
from the published prices above and say so. Hosted Bunny charges were under
investigation on 2026-11-01; the test lead reconciles them first and records
the measured per-run cost here. Reconciliation records unknown attribution as
unknown; it does not pause authorized essential testing. The prior first-pair
run has no independently attributable before/after billing measurement yet.

Before any hosted run, the run first proves that the deployed build equals the
commit under test. Every tool that sends requests to a hosted copy calls
`tools/hosted-build-guard/assert-hosted-build-matches-commit.mjs` first: it reads
the deployed build's identity from the host's `/_status` (one GET) and refuses
with exit 1, before any test request, when that identity is missing, unreadable,
built from uncommitted changes, or not the commit the run claims to test. The
deploy step stamps the commit into each bundle (`tools/hosted-build-guard/build-stamp.mjs`),
so a bundle without a stamp cannot pass.

## What qualifies as an essential hosted run

A hosted run is essential only when all of these hold:

1. It checks something no local test can (real Bunny runtime, real hosted
   database, real CDN behavior, or the Ruby-vs-Bunny comparison on shared data).
2. The code or configuration it checks changed since the last hosted run of
   the same test, or it has never run. No feel-good repeats.
3. Its result changes a decision (accept or reject a fix, or release).

## Batching and reruns

- Batch: collect queued requests and run them together in the fewest runs.
  The full Ruby-vs-Bunny comparison runs once, as one planned run, after the
  pending fixes land.
- Never rerun the same hosted test without a code change that justifies it;
  name the commit in the log.
- Money is never a reason to stop work. If hosted testing looks too
  expensive, the tests are wasteful: make them cheaper, batched or local.

